//! The flat build profile of the riscv32 `virt` board: the tier that denies
//! nothing.
//!
//! Same architecture, board and toolchain as the isolated profile. The one
//! difference is that this profile programs the hardware to deny nothing, and
//! says so through the tier word. Everything else (trap frame, UART, CLINT
//! timer, PLIC, memory map, linker scripts, user-side ABI) comes from the two
//! modules that already own it, so there is no second copy to keep in step.
//!
//! ## The three modules
//!
//! ```
//! rv32core   hal/riscv32-common/kernel   the arch: frame, PMP, cause decode
//! rv32virt   hal/riscv32/kernel          the board: UART, CLINT, PLIC, map
//! hal        hal/riscv32-flat/kernel     this file: the protection profile
//! ```
//!
//! `rv32virt` is the virt board's own `lib.saw` under a second module name.
//! The runner maps `rv32virt=hal/riscv32/kernel` for this profile and
//! `hal=hal/riscv32/kernel` for the isolated one. A Saw module's name comes
//! from its `--module-path` mapping, so one directory can be the board's `hal`
//! in one build and a component of another `hal` in a second.
//!
//! The arch names are taken from `rv32core` directly rather than through
//! `rv32virt`, which re-exports them too. The names this profile must not
//! inherit are the protection ones, so naming the arch half makes the omission
//! visible in one list instead of hiding it in a chain.
//!
//! ## What "flat" means
//!
//! `prot_isolated()` answers `false` and `SystemOp.TierGet` answers `Flat`.
//!
//! - `map` succeeds honestly. Its contract is "make this region accessible
//!   with at least these rights", and every region already is. The kernel
//!   still records the row, charges the quota and hands back a Mapping that
//!   owns it.
//! - `unmap`'s revocation half is disclaimed. The row leaves the record and
//!   the Mapping dies; the hardware goes on permitting the access.
//! - Peer isolation is disclaimed. A process can reach another's memory.
//! - Protecting the kernel's memory is disclaimed too. The U-mode range is
//!   read/write/execute over the whole address space, the kernel and the test
//!   finisher included, so a process that writes kernel memory directly
//!   bypasses every check (`SystemRight.Shutdown` included).
//! - The checks on the syscall path are not disclaimed. Handles, rights,
//!   quotas and lifetimes are checked on the syscall path exactly as on the
//!   isolated profile, so the object-model suite runs unchanged here.
//!
//! ## PMP is opened, not left alone
//!
//! RISC-V denies by default: if no PMP entry matches a U-mode access and at
//! least one entry is implemented, the access fails. At reset every `pmpcfg`
//! byte is zero (A=OFF), so a profile that emptied the `prot_*` bodies would
//! deny root its own first instruction. Flat therefore installs one TOR entry
//! spanning the whole address space, RWX, and that is the only hardware this
//! file touches.
//!
//! It is published from both `prot_commit` and `prot_switch`, which are
//! stateless and idempotent. `load_domain` calls `prot_switch`
//! unconditionally; a harness kernel under `tests/` that programs the seam
//! directly ends at `prot_commit`. Covering both needs no extra HAL hook.
//!
//! The register writes live in `rv32core.prot_open_all`, beside `prot_reset`,
//! because PMP is architectural. This file decides when the hardware is
//! opened; `rv32core` knows how.

// The arch half. Not taken from `rv32core`: its seven protection functions
// (`prot_reset`, `prot_region`, `prot_device`, `prot_commit`, `prot_switch`,
// `prot_update` and `prot_clear`), defined below instead; `prot_open_all`,
// imported plainly below; and `INTERRUPT_FLAG`, `InterruptCause` and
// `WORD_BITS`, which only board code uses. The two tier facts this file also
// defines, `prot_replay_at_switch` and `prot_isolated`, are `rv32virt` names.
// That makes nine `prot_*` functions here.
//
// `PROT_GRAIN`, `GRANT_ROW_BUDGET`, `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` and `device_window_ok`
// come across unchanged. The kernel's static asserts price a grant record
// against these numbers, and a profile that moved them would be testing a
// different kernel. `device_window_ok` still refuses a malformed window here:
// the flat tier disclaims memory protection, not the kernel's argument checks
// on the syscall path.
public import rv32core.{
    arch_tag, cause_tag, device_window_ok, FRAME_BYTES, frame_init,
    GRANT_ROW_BUDGET, image_link_base, IRQ_NONE, IRQ_TIMER, is_interrupt,
    is_syscall, map_place,
    MAX_ROOT_SEGMENTS, payload_end, payload_start, phys_to_virt,
    PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS, PROT_GRAIN,
    region_table_end, region_table_start,
    resume_frame, syscall_arg0, syscall_arg1, syscall_arg2, syscall_handle,
    syscall_op, syscall_return, trap_pc, virt_to_phys,
}

// The board half, re-exported from the virt board itself. Each of these is a
// fact about the QEMU `virt` machine (UART, CLINT, PLIC context, grant windows,
// link address) and none changes with the protection profile.
//
// Board names not taken: `arch_name`, because this profile announces itself
// below so a transcript diff can tell the two profiles apart; the two tier
// facts, which this file answers; `RAM_BASE` and `RAM_TOP`, which only the
// board's own map predicates read; and `rt_write` and `rt_abort`, which are
// `@export`ed and reach the runtime by symbol, not by module.
public import rv32virt.{
    console_byte, DEVICE_GRANT_BASE, DEVICE_GRANT_LEN, exit_fail, exit_pass,
    intc_init, irq_claim, irq_complete, irq_line_valid, irq_mask, irq_poll,
    irq_raise_selftest_line, irq_unmask, map_source_ok, map_target_ok,
    ROOT_LOAD_BASE, ROOT_REGION_TOP, ROOT_STACK_LEN, timer_now_ns,
    timer_pending, timer_set_deadline_ns, UNMAPPED_PROBE, wait_for_irq,
}

// `prot_open_all` belongs to `rv32core` (PMP is architectural) and is reached
// through a plain import, not re-exported: `kernel/core` sees only the
// `prot_*` functions below, and opening the hardware is how this profile
// implements `prot_commit` and `prot_switch`.
//
// It must stay in one module: the CSR writes are `extern "C"` leaves, an extern
// carries no visibility and cannot be re-exported, and two modules declaring
// one C symbol collide across the compilation unit.
import rv32core.{prot_open_all}

// =============================================================================
// Identity
// =============================================================================

/// What the boot banner says. It names the profile.
///
/// The harness matches its banner expectation as a prefix, so the arch word at
/// the front is what the assertion reads and the parenthesis is for the human
/// reading a transcript.
public func arch_name() -> String {
    "riscv32 (QEMU virt, flat profile)"
}

// =============================================================================
// The protection seam — the whole of this profile
// =============================================================================

/// Whether this profile rebuilds its protection image at every switch. It
/// doesn't: there is nothing to rebuild. (A function and not a `static`: a
/// `static` is a name the whole program shares, so a profile could not
/// override one.)
///
/// `false` retires `load_domain`'s replay loop: the kernel keeps its grant
/// record (teardown and compaction still walk it, and the quota ledger still
/// prices it) and never programs hardware from it. The constant folds, so the
/// loop is not emitted.
public func prot_replay_at_switch() -> Bool { false }

/// Whether this platform can deny. It can't.
///
/// `kernel/core` maps this to `sosabi.ProtectionTier.Flat` and
/// `SystemOp.TierGet` hands it to userspace, where a program that relies on
/// isolation reads it once and refuses to run. The tier is advertised, never
/// faked: every `prot_*` body here is empty or permissive, and this word is
/// what makes that honest.
public func prot_isolated() -> Bool { false }

/// Nothing to reset: this profile installs one permission and never varies
/// it.
public func prot_reset() unsafe {
}

/// A no-op. The grant row stays in the kernel's record, which is authoritative
/// at every tier; this profile never turns a row into a hardware region.
public func prot_region(idx: UInt, base: UInt, top: UInt, perms: UInt8) unsafe {
}

/// A no-op, for `prot_region`'s reason.
public func prot_device(idx: UInt, base: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
}

/// Publish the permissive configuration. One of the two places
/// `rv32core.prot_open_all` is reached from.
///
/// A harness kernel under `tests/` that programs `prot_reset`/`prot_region`/
/// `prot_commit` directly, rather than through `load_domain`, ends its
/// protection setup with this call. Without it, such a kernel would have a dead
/// PMP and a U-mode payload that faults on its first instruction.
public func prot_commit() unsafe {
    prot_open_all()
}

/// Publish the permissive configuration. The other place, and the one the real
/// kernel takes.
///
/// `load_domain` calls this unconditionally, outside the
/// `prot_replay_at_switch()` guard, so it runs before root enters U-mode and
/// again at every process switch. Redoing the work each time keeps this
/// profile stateless: there is no "already opened" word to get wrong.
public func prot_switch(p: UInt) unsafe {
    prot_open_all()
}

/// A no-op apart from one assertion.
///
/// The flat tier does not translate, so a user address is its physical
/// address. A row whose `pa` drifted from its `base` would mean the placement
/// seams disagree, and a profile programming no hardware could not otherwise
/// notice.
public func prot_update(p: UInt, base: UInt, top: UInt, pa: UInt, perms: UInt8,
                        device: Bool) unsafe {
    assert(pa == base,
           "a user address on the flat tier IS its physical address")
}

/// Nothing to retire. `clear_domain` has already emptied the kernel's record,
/// the only place this profile's protection state lives.
public func prot_clear(p: UInt) unsafe {
}
